# Docker via Make (no host runtimes)

All Rosey product repos ship Docker Compose + Makefile targets. Agents must run apps and tooling **inside containers**, not on the host.

## Order of preference

1. **`make <target>`** — preferred for every supported operation
2. **`docker compose …`** — only when no Make target covers the need
3. **Never** run app/runtime commands directly on the host

## Forbidden on the host

- `npm` / `yarn` / `pnpm` / `npx` (install, run, test, lint, build)
- `pip` / `pip3` / `uv pip` / `python -m pip` / host `virtualenv` for project deps
- `node`, `python`, `python3`, `go run`, `cargo` for project app code
- Editing or creating host site-packages / global tool installs for project work
- “Host-only” shortcuts that bypass Compose when Docker is available

## Use Make equivalents

Check the repo `Makefile` first. Common mappings:

| Intent | Prefer |
| ------ | ------ |
| Install deps | `make dependencies` |
| Build images | `make image` |
| Start services | `make start` |
| Run app(s) | `make serve` / `make serve-backend` / `make serve-frontend` |
| Lint | `make lint` |
| Format | `make format` |
| Test | `make test` |
| Shell in container | `make console` (when defined) |

If a target name differs, use the repo’s Makefile — do not invent host commands.

## When Make has no target

Use Compose against the repo’s compose file(s), e.g.:

```bash
docker compose run --rm <service> <command>
docker compose exec <service> <command>
```

Still never install Node/Python deps on the host; install/run inside the service container.

## Exceptions

- Git, `gh`, editor/IDE, and Hindsight (`hindsight-embed`) stay on the host.
- `brew install` (and other Homebrew commands) are allowed on the host for machine tooling, but **confirm with the user before running** any `brew` install/upgrade/uninstall.
- Host package managers only when the user explicitly asks to change the machine itself (Docker Engine, system packages) — not for app dependencies.
